# Blog Post Conventions

> **`@hyper-fetch/testing` is internal.** Never mention, recommend or list it here. It is not published and not part of
> the public API. Mocking for users is `request.setMock()`.

## BlogPostImage is required

Every blog post (tutorial, release, feature) must use the `<BlogPostImage>` component as its hero image. Import it from
`@site/src/components`. No raw `<img>` tags or markdown images for the post hero.

```mdx
import { BlogPostImage } from "@site/src/components";

<BlogPostImage
  title="Post Title"
  description="Short tagline."
  badge="tutorial"
  href="/blog/slug"
  pattern="grid"
/>
```

## Pattern variety

Available patterns: `dots`, `grid`, `hexagon`, `striped`, `flickering`, `retro`, `interactive`, `paths`.

**Patterns must not repeat across consecutive posts.** When writing a new post, check the most recent 2-3 posts and pick
a different pattern. Rotate through the full set over time.

Exception: release posts always use `paths` (see below).

## Pattern masks

Control how the pattern fades at the edges via `patternMask`. Available masks:

| Mask | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `radial` (default) | Radial gradient fade, positioned by `patternFocus` (`top` / `center` / `bottom`) |
| `full` | No mask, pattern fills the entire area |
| `top` | Hides top, pattern visible toward bottom |
| `bottom` | Hides bottom, pattern visible toward top |
| `sides` | Hides left and right sides, visible in center |
| `left` | Hides left side, pattern visible toward right |
| `right` | Hides right side, pattern visible toward left |
| `rectangle` | Visible only in a center rectangle, fades on all four edges |

Vary masks across posts the same way you vary patterns - avoid repeating the same mask on consecutive posts.

## Post-type rules

### Tutorials (`badge="tutorial"`)

- Can include a short code snippet as `<BlogPostImage>` children (renders as a terminal window).
- Pick any pattern except `paths` (reserved for releases). Vary the choice.

### Releases (`badge="release"`)

- **Always use `pattern="paths"`** to match the hero section aesthetic.
- **Do NOT include code as children.** The post body carries the code, section by section.
- Follow the `release-posts` skill (`.claude/skills/release-posts/SKILL.md`): it has the formula for major hub posts,
  area deep dives and minor releases, and a script that checks the rendered posts.

```mdx
<BlogPostImage
  title="Hyper Fetch 9.0"
  description="Faster. Smarter. Typed."
  badge="release"
  href="/blog/hyper-fetch-9-0"
/>
```

### Features (`badge="feature"`)

- Pick any pattern except `paths`. Vary the choice.
- Can optionally include code children if a short snippet captures the feature.

## Release and feature post structure

Readers scan a release post for the one change they care about, then read its code. The `release-posts` skill has the
full formula, learned from the Docusaurus, Vite, Next.js, Astro, TanStack Query and React release posts. In short:

1. **Intro**, 2-5 sentences before `{/* truncate */}`: what changed, one concrete hook, and the upgrade cost
   ("upgrading needs no code changes").
2. **Version note**, area posts only, one line: `:::info Version` with "Written for **HyperFetch X**" and a link to the
   release overview.
3. **`## Highlights`** as a bullet list, never cards: `- **[Feature name](#section-anchor)**: one line on the gain.`
   Anchors follow Docusaurus heading ids: lowercase, spaces become dashes, punctuation is dropped
   (`## Node.js, Bun and Deno` is `#nodejs-bun-and-deno`). In a release overview, a highlight may point at an area post.
4. **Sections**, one per change, split by `---`.
5. **Reliability fixes**, grouped by `###` area when there are more than about eight.
6. **Upgrading**, then **Learn more** as a plain list of links, one line each. No `LinkCard` and no `<Highlights>`
   card grid in release or feature posts: a link costs one line at most.

### Sections

- Open with one sentence that states the gain. Put the problem in the same sentence or the next, not in a paragraph
  of its own.
- Then the shortest code that shows the change, at most about 15 lines. Cut imports, client setup and types the reader
  can infer. Split a long block into two focused ones, or use `<Tabs>` (client and server, React and core, two
  options).
- Then at most a short paragraph, or a bullet list or a small table for options, defaults and behavior rules.
- Headings are noun phrases in sentence case. No question headings.
- `:::tip` and `:::warning` only for a real gotcha, never for a summary.

### Code blocks

- Blog CSS tints highlighted lines gold and dims the rest until hover, so **every code block that shows a feature marks
  the lines that are the point** with `// highlight-next-line`, `// highlight-start` / `// highlight-end`, or a `{3-5}`
  metastring (use the metastring for JSON, which has no comments). Setup and boilerplate stay unmarked. Never highlight
  a whole block.
- Add `title="file.ts"` when a filename helps orientation.
- Keep code lines within about 80 characters, so blocks do not scroll sideways.

### Breaking changes and upgrading

- Open with a table when there are several changes: `Change | Before | After`, or `Change | What to do`.
- Show each change as a `Before` / `After` `<Tabs>` pair, with the new line highlighted in `After`. A `diff-add` /
  `diff-remove` block also works for a one-line change.
- Say who is affected and when nothing needs to change.

### Writing

Load the `technical-writing`, `copy-editing` and `humanizer` skills from `.claude/skills/` before writing or editing a
post. In short: conclusion first, short sentences, active voice, no em dashes, no "it's not X, it's Y", no "no X, no
Y, no Z" triads, no staged openers ("Here's the thing"), no filler adverbs ("seamlessly"), and no closer that repeats
the section. Verify every API name, option and default against `packages/*/src`.
